cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "AZ-Eval: a parallel Azerbaijani-English benchmark"
message: "If you use this benchmark, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Garibli
    given-names: Nihat
    email: qaribli.nihat.nemat@bsu.edu.az
    affiliation: Baku State University
repository-code: "https://github.com/nihatgaribli/AZ-Eval-benchmark"
abstract: >-
  An open parallel Azerbaijani-English benchmark of 1006 human-verified
  short-answer items, 708 of them a control stratum whose subject matter is
  universal by construction, with an evaluation pipeline that separates
  knowledge from orthography through a chain of nested normalizations.
  Used to show that what fine-tuning on a neighbouring language costs
  Azerbaijani is predicted by the script of the adaptation target and not by
  its relatedness. Across nine measured pairs, relatedness splits exactly
  evenly; every Latin-script target leaves Azerbaijani intact, and four of
  five Cyrillic-script targets damage it by 8 to 11 points beyond general
  forgetting. Turkish, the closest relative, costs nothing; Russian, entirely
  unrelated, costs 8 to 10 points.
keywords:
  - low-resource evaluation
  - Azerbaijani
  - cross-lingual transfer
  - Turkic languages
  - writing systems
  - large language models
license: CC-BY-4.0
date-released: "2026-08-07"
